    {
     "@type": "Question",
     "name": "Why are my spider plant's leaf tips turning brown?",
     "acceptedAnswer": {
      "@type": "Answer",
      "text": "Brown tips are a common spider plant problem, and they have several causes. UW–Madison Extension lists low humidity, very dry soil, salt build-up and chemicals in water, especially fluoride and chlorine. Clemson Extension adds too much fertiliser and water high in soluble salts. Check the water and the soil first."
     }
    },
    {
     "@type": "Question",
     "name": "Why are my spider plant leaves turning yellow and limp?",
     "acceptedAnswer": {
      "@type": "Answer",
      "text": "Yellow, limp spider plant leaves usually point to a watering problem. Clemson Extension says yellowing is most often a symptom of overwatering, and that root rot comes from soil that does not drain quickly or from excessive watering. Feel the soil: if it is wet, the roots may be rotting."
     }
    },
    {
     "@type": "Question",
     "name": "Why is my spider plant not producing babies?",
     "acceptedAnswer": {
      "@type": "Answer",
      "text": "A spider plant makes babies in response to short days and long, uninterrupted nights for at least three weeks (NC State Extension). Heavy feeding reduces plantlets (NC State Extension, UW–Madison Extension), and plants produce them best when slightly pot-bound (UW–Madison Extension)."
     }
    },
    {
     "@type": "Question",
     "name": "Why does my spider plant have scorched patches?",
     "acceptedAnswer": {
      "@type": "Answer",
      "text": "Scorched patches on a spider plant are a sign of too much direct sun. NC State Extension calls the plant intolerant of direct sunlight, Clemson Extension warns that midday sun may scorch the leaves, and UW–Madison Extension says it can sunburn in full sun outdoors. In the UAE this often happens near west- or south-facing glass or on a balcony."
     }
    },
    {
     "@type": "Question",
     "name": "What are the bumps or white cotton on my spider plant?",
     "acceptedAnswer": {
      "@type": "Answer",
      "text": "Small brown bumps or white cottony clusters on a spider plant are most likely scale insects or mealybugs, which UW–Madison Extension names as the main pests. Clemson Extension also lists whiteflies, spider mites and aphids."
     }
    },
    {
     "@type": "Question",
     "name": "Why are thick roots pushing my spider plant out of its pot?",
     "acceptedAnswer": {
      "@type": "Answer",
      "text": "A spider plant grows thick, fleshy tuberous roots (Clemson Extension). When they fill the pot, they push the plant up and can crack the container. Clemson Extension advises dividing and repotting before that happens."
     }
    },
    {
     "@type": "Question",
     "name": "Can a spider plant live in low light?",
     "acceptedAnswer": {
      "@type": "Answer",
      "text": "Yes. NC State Extension says a spider plant prefers medium light but can tolerate deep shade. Clemson Extension says it grows best in bright indirect light."
     }
    },
    {
     "@type": "Question",
     "name": "Does a spider plant clean the air?",
     "acceptedAnswer": {
      "@type": "Answer",
      "text": "A spider plant does not noticeably clean the air in a normal room. The studies that found plants removing chemicals were done in small sealed chambers. A 2019 review by Cummings and Waring concluded that a building would need 10 to 1,000 plants per square metre of floor to match normal air exchange. Buy a spider plant for its looks, not as an air filter."
     }
    },
    {
     "@type": "Question",
     "name": "Can a spider plant grow outside in the UAE?",
     "acceptedAnswer": {
      "@type": "Answer",
      "text": "A spider plant is best kept indoors in the UAE. UW–Madison Extension says that outdoors it needs bright light but can sunburn in full sun, and that in warm climates it is grown in partly shaded areas. UAE summer heat is far above the range Clemson Extension gives as ideal. A shaded outdoor spot is possible in the cooler months on the coast; bring it in before summer and before cold inland nights."
     }
    },
    {
     "@type": "Question",
     "name": "How big does a spider plant get?",
     "acceptedAnswer": {
      "@type": "Answer",
      "text": "A spider plant grows about 30–45 cm tall (NC State Extension, UW–Madison Extension). It spreads wider than it is tall: Clemson Extension says it reaches about 60–75 cm wide and 60–90 cm long in a hanging basket."
     }
    },
    {
     "@type": "Question",
     "name": "Do spider plants flower?",
     "acceptedAnswer": {
      "@type": "Answer",
      "text": "Yes. A spider plant grows small, white, star-shaped flowers on its long stems, and the babies form on the same stems (NC State Extension). Flowers usually appear after short days and long nights, but indoors they can appear at any time of year (UW–Madison Extension)."
     }
    },
    {
     "@type": "Question",
     "name": "Why are the stripes on my spider plant fading?",
     "acceptedAnswer": {
      "@type": "Answer",
      "text": "Fading stripes are normal in some spider plant cultivars. NC State Extension says 'Hawaiian' shows variegation on new leaves that fades with age, and UW–Madison Extension says the narrow white line of 'White Stripe' is lost as the leaf ages."
     }
    },
    {
     "@type": "Question",
     "name": "How is plant size measured?",
     "acceptedAnswer": {
      "@type": "Answer",
      "text": "Size is measured from the bottom of the nursery pot to the top tip of the highest leaf. A plant listed as 50-60 CM stands in that range measured this way."
     }
    },
    {
     "@type": "Question",
     "name": "Which pot option should I choose, nursery or decorative?",
     "acceptedAnswer": {
      "@type": "Answer",
      "text": "Choose the nursery pot for the lowest price or if you already have a planter. Choose a decorative pot for a ready-to-display plant that arrives fully potted with soil mix, perlite and a drainage layer. The plant itself is the same."
     }
    },
    {
     "@type": "Question",
     "name": "Do the decorative pots have drainage holes?",
     "acceptedAnswer": {
      "@type": "Answer",
      "text": "Decorative pot orders include a drainage layer at the base, with premium soil mix and perlite. For specific details on a particular pot model, send us the product link and our team will confirm."
     }
    },
    {
     "@type": "Question",
     "name": "Is the plant healthy on arrival? Can I check first?",
     "acceptedAnswer": {
      "@type": "Answer",
      "text": "Check your plant with the delivery agent: if it's damaged or not what you ordered, you can refuse it on the spot. If you find damage after unpacking, send photos and your order number on WhatsApp (+971 4 294 1422) or email sales@plantsworld.ae within 24 hours and we'll replace it free. You can also ask for a pre-dispatch photo."
     }
    },
    {
     "@type": "Question",
     "name": "My plant arrived with a few drooping or yellow leaves. Is that normal?",
     "acceptedAnswer": {
      "@type": "Answer",
      "text": "Yes, that's normal. A few drooping, bent or yellow leaves are a common reaction to the journey and a new spot, and plants usually recover within a few days. Keep it in bright indirect light and check the soil before watering. If the plant or pot is actually damaged, send photos within 24 hours."
     }
    },
    {
     "@type": "Question",
     "name": "Will my plant be safe in the UAE heat during delivery?",
     "acceptedAnswer": {
      "@type": "Answer",
      "text": "Yes. Our own delivery team brings your order, not a third-party courier. In extreme summer heat (June to September) we may move deliveries to farther emirates to a cooler day and agree the new day with you. If anything arrives damaged, we replace it free."
     }
    },
    {
     "@type": "Question",
     "name": "Can I send a plant as a gift to someone?",
